# Cheatsheet

## Verify
{: .col-2}

### A whole charter

```elixir
alias CharterAgreementProtocol, as: CAP
alias CharterAgreementProtocol.Limits

{:ok, facts} =
  CAP.verify_chain(
    revisions,     # [binary] canonical revision bytes
    acceptances,   # [binary] compact JWS
    descriptors,   # [binary] compact JWS, every party's history
    terminations,  # [binary] compact JWS
    Limits.default()
  )
```

Every argument is a list of raw bytes. Pass `[]` when there are no terminations.

### Which revision is in force

```elixir
CAP.governing_revision(facts, ~U[2026-09-01 00:00:00Z])
#=> {:ok, "sha-256:..."}   # one revision
#=> {:ok, :contested}      # two valid revisions compete
#=> {:ok, :none}           # nothing in force (not yet, or terminated)
```

### One artifact

```elixir
CAP.verify_descriptor(compact, predecessor_or_nil, limits)
CAP.verify_descriptor_chain(compacts, limits)
CAP.decode_charter_revision(bytes, limits)
CAP.verify_acceptance(compact, revision, descriptor_chain, limits)
CAP.verify_termination(compact, revision, descriptor_chain, limits)
CAP.verify_receipt(compact, chain_facts_or_revision, limits)
```

### Narrow what you accept

```elixir
{:ok, profile} =
  CharterAgreementProtocol.Capability.Profile.new(
    algorithms: ["Ed25519"],
    revisions: {2, 2}
  )

CAP.verify_chain(revisions, acceptances, descriptors, terminations,
                 Limits.default(), profile)
```

An artifact outside the profile fails with `:algorithm_outside_profile` or
`:revision_outside_profile` before its own signature is checked.

## Produce
{: .col-2}

### Revision bytes from a plain map

```elixir
{:ok, revision_bytes} = CAP.encode_charter_revision(claims)
{:ok, revision} = CAP.decode_charter_revision(revision_bytes, Limits.default())
CAP.revision_digest(revision)
#=> "sha-256:..."
```

### Bytes to sign, then the compact

```elixir
{:ok, input} =
  CAP.descriptor_signing_input(%{"kid" => kid, "claims" => claims})

signature = your_signer.(input.message)
{:ok, compact} = CAP.assemble_compact(input, signature)
```

`"algorithm"` is optional: `"Ed25519"` (the default, claims at
`protocol_revision` 2) or `"ML-DSA-65"` (`protocol_revision` 3). The signature is
64 bytes for Ed25519 and 3309 for ML-DSA-65.

### Check before signing

```elixir
alias CharterAgreementProtocol.Capability.Profile

input = %{"kid" => kid, "claims" => claims, "public_key" => public_key}

{:ok, plan} =
  CAP.prepare_signing(:acceptance, input, set, Limits.default(), Profile.full())

signature = your_signer.(plan.signing_input.message)
{:ok, compact} = CAP.finish_signing(plan, signature)
```

Kinds: `:party_descriptor` (context `nil` or predecessor facts), `:acceptance` and
`:termination` (an `ArtifactSet`), `:receipt` (`ChainFacts` or a bare revision). A
refusal arrives before any signature exists: `:signing_key_mismatch` when the key is
not the holder's, or the code verification would return.

### Set-aware producers

```elixir
{:ok, set} = CAP.build_set(revisions, acceptances, terminations, descriptors)
CAP.acceptance_signing_input(%{"kid" => kid, "claims" => claims}, set)
CAP.termination_signing_input(%{"kid" => kid, "claims" => claims}, set)
CAP.receipt_signing_input(%{"kid" => kid, "claims" => claims})
```

Note the argument order: `build_set/4` takes terminations before descriptors;
`verify_chain/5` takes descriptors before terminations. The producers return
`{:error, %Error{code: :signing_refused}}` when the claims conflict with the set.

## Results
{: .col-2}

### What a result never proves

Every facts record has `not_verified`, always these twelve:

```elixir
[:tenancy, :live_policy, :authority, :effect_ownership, :execution, :billing,
 :evaluation_truth, :legal_validity, :term_satisfaction, :view_completeness,
 :counterparty_view, :wall_clock]
```

### Errors

```elixir
{:error, %CharterAgreementProtocol.Error{code: code, subject: subject}}
```

`code` is an atom from a closed list. `subject` names where it failed. Neither
contains the input, so errors are safe to log. See
[Handle errors](error-codes.md).

### What this runtime can verify

```elixir
CAP.capabilities()
#=> %{algorithms: [%{name: "EdDSA", key_algorithm: "Ed25519", verifiable: true}, ...],
#     linked_crypto: ...}
```

A well-formed artifact whose algorithm is marked `verifiable: false` fails with
`:algorithm_unsupported_on_substrate`: the signature could not be checked. A
signature of the wrong length still fails with `:signature_invalid` first.

## Limits and the CLI
{: .col-2}

### Limits

| Field | Default | Maximum |
|---|---|---|
| `max_bytes` | 1,048,576 | 16,777,216 |
| `max_depth` | 64 | 128 |
| `max_object_members` | 1,024 | 65,536 |
| `max_array_items` | 4,096 | 65,536 |
| `max_string_bytes` | 65,536 | 1,048,576 |
| `max_artifact_set_items` (per list) | 1,024 | 4,096 |
| `max_artifact_set_bytes` (all lists) | 67,108,864 | 1,073,741,824 |

```elixir
{:ok, limits} = Limits.new(max_artifact_set_items: 64)
```

### Check the shipped corpus

```console
$ mix run -e 'System.halt(CharterAgreementProtocol.Conformance.Cli.run(["--corpus", "deps/charter_agreement_protocol/priv/conformance"]))'
```

Exit status `0` when every case agrees, `1` when any disagrees or the corpus fails
to load, `2` for a usage error.
